\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Static conventional data analysis workflow vs.\ dynamic \gls {gpm} generated workflow}. \DIFdelbeginFL  {\color {red}\sout@orig {The chemical }}\DIFdelendFL  \DIFaddbeginFL  {\color {blue}\uwave {Chemical }}\DIFaddendFL  analysis can be \DIFdelbeginFL  {\color {red}\sout@orig {done }}\DIFdelendFL  \DIFaddbeginFL  {\color {blue}\uwave {performed }}\DIFaddendFL  with a variety of \DIFdelbeginFL  {\color {red}\sout@orig {possible }}\DIFdelendFL  instruments and techniques, resulting in \DIFdelbeginFL  {\color {red}\sout@orig {a large number of }}\DIFdelendFL  \DIFaddbeginFL  {\color {blue}\uwave {many }}\DIFaddendFL  possible output data formats. The \gls {gpm} can use these diverse, raw data and process \DIFdelbeginFL  {\color {red}\sout@orig {it }}\DIFdelendFL  \DIFaddbeginFL  {\color {blue}\uwave {them }}\DIFaddendFL  into easy-to-understand plots, analysis\DIFaddbeginFL  {\color {blue}\uwave {, }}\DIFaddendFL  and reports. A hard-coded workflow, in contrast, is specifically made to analyze one specific data format and spectra and produces a fixed output format, e.g., the \gls {smiles} of the analyzed molecule.}}{99}{figure.caption.75}\protected@file@percent }
\@gls@reference{acronym}{gpm}{\glsnoidxdisplayloc{}{page}{glsnumberformat}{99}}
