\newlabel{fig:anaylsis}{{17}{99}{\textbf {Static conventional data analysis workflow vs.\ dynamic \gls {gpm} generated workflow}. \DIFdelbeginFL \DIFdelFL {The chemical }\DIFdelendFL \DIFaddbeginFL \DIFaddFL {Chemical }\DIFaddendFL analysis can be \DIFdelbeginFL \DIFdelFL {done }\DIFdelendFL \DIFaddbeginFL \DIFaddFL {performed }\DIFaddendFL with a variety of \DIFdelbeginFL \DIFdelFL {possible }\DIFdelendFL instruments and techniques, resulting in \DIFdelbeginFL \DIFdelFL {a large number of }\DIFdelendFL \DIFaddbeginFL \DIFaddFL {many }\DIFaddendFL possible output data formats. The \gls {gpm} can use these diverse, raw data and process \DIFdelbeginFL \DIFdelFL {it }\DIFdelendFL \DIFaddbeginFL \DIFaddFL {them }\DIFaddendFL into easy-to-understand plots, analysis\DIFaddbeginFL \DIFaddFL {, }\DIFaddendFL and reports. A hard-coded workflow, in contrast, is specifically made to analyze one specific data format and spectra and produces a fixed output format, e.g., the \gls {smiles} of the analyzed molecule}{figure.caption.75}{}}
